      body: '{{img:0}}<h3>What Omaha homeowners actually pay</h3><p>Omaha prices from HomeBlue’s Omaha guide, flat prices from Thumbtack’s Omaha page, and national job prices labeled national:</p><table class=\"price-table\"><thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Typical price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Handyman, 1 hour, Omaha (HomeBlue)</td><td>$40–$80</td></tr><tr><td>Handyman, 2 hours, Omaha (HomeBlue)</td><td>$80–$150</td></tr><tr><td>Handyman, 4 hours (half day), Omaha (HomeBlue)</td><td>$160–$310</td></tr><tr><td>Handyman, 8 hours (full day), Omaha (HomeBlue)</td><td>$320–$610</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum rate for a small job (Thumbtack, Omaha page)</td><td>$60–$100</td></tr><tr><td>Faucet replacement, flat (Thumbtack, Omaha page)</td><td>$125</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling fan or light fixture, flat (Thumbtack, Omaha page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Toilet replacement, flat (Thumbtack, Omaha page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Garbage disposal, flat (Thumbtack, Omaha page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Gutter cleaning, Omaha (HomeBlue)</td><td>$60–$210</td></tr><tr><td>Leaf removal, Omaha (HomeBlue)</td><td>$40–$280</td></tr><tr><td>Drywall repair (HomeGuide, national)</td><td>$200–$800</td></tr><tr><td>TV mounting (HomeGuide, national)</td><td>$100–$300</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is where people overpay. A 20-minute fix booked on its own pays the minimum, plus the drive across town. The same fix on a half-day list — a faucet, a disposal, a patch, a sticking door — just takes its share of the $160–$310. Nationally, minimums run $75–$200 with a $30–$80 trip charge on top for some pros (HomeGuide, national). Omaha’s floor sits lower, but the math is the same: one trip should carry as much work as you can hand over.</p>{{img:1}}<h3>What changes the price in Omaha?</h3><p><strong>The list, or the lack of one.</strong> Write the small jobs down as they happen. In West Omaha (68144), one visit can cover a running toilet, a loose railing, and the gutters. Booked one at a time, each one pays its own minimum.</p><p><strong>November is gutter and leaf season here.</strong> Big trees in West Omaha and Northwest Omaha (68142) drop a lot of leaves, and full gutters freeze that way. Gutter cleaning in Omaha runs $60–$210, priced at $0.50–$0.90 a linear foot (HomeBlue, Omaha). Leaf removal runs $40–$280, and raking alone runs $30–$50 an hour (HomeBlue, Omaha). Both are cheaper now than after the first snow sits on them.</p><p><strong>Nebraska registers contractors, it does not license handymen.</strong> There is no state handyman license. But anyone doing more than $5,000 of construction or repair work in a year has to register with the Nebraska Department of Labor, and electrical, plumbing, and HVAC work carry their own licenses. So a handyman can patch, hang, assemble, and repair — a panel rewire or a gas line is a licensed trade’s job. Some Omaha work also needs a permit; HomeBlue notes an exterior door replacement sometimes does, and decks of a certain size and height can too.</p><p><strong>Who you hire.</strong> Nationally, a self-employed handyman runs $50–$80 an hour and a company handyman runs $75–$125 (HomeGuide, national). Omaha’s $40–$80 range covers both ends of that. A company crew costs more and usually guarantees the work. A solo operator costs less and books out faster in fall.</p><h3>How to pay less without a worse job</h3><ul class=\"check-list\"><li><strong>Bundle the list.</strong> One four-hour visit at $160–$310 beats four separate calls, each hitting a $60–$100 minimum.</li><li><strong>Get the fall work done before snow.</strong> Gutters at $60–$210 and leaves at $40–$280 are November prices. Ice and a ladder in January are a different quote.</li><li><strong>Buy the parts yourself when you can.</strong> A handyman who buys materials may add a 20% to 50% markup, plus a trip charge (HomeGuide, national). The faucet or disposal sitting by the door removes both.</li><li><strong>Ask the price shape before he starts.</strong> Hourly, flat per job, or hourly plus a minimum — all three are normal in Omaha. Not knowing which one you agreed to is where most bad bills come from.</li></ul><h3>An Omaha handyman listed on Van Squads</h3><p><strong>Ace Handyman Services Omaha</strong> is listed on Van Squads, offering handyman work at your location in Omaha ZIPs 68144 and 68124. See its listing: <a href=\"/business/ace-handyman-services-omaha\">Ace Handyman Services Omaha on Van Squads</a>.</p><p>Van Squads lists 12 handyman businesses in the Omaha metro. Coverage is heaviest in West Omaha: eight listings cover ZIP 68144, five cover 68124, two cover 68122, and the rest reach 68135, Northwest Omaha 68142, and 68154: <a href=\"/#/browse?zip=68144\">search handyman near 68144</a>.</p><p>Want the whole list on one page? <a href=\"/handyman/omaha/\">Handyman in Omaha</a> has every company Van Squads lists there, with phone numbers and service areas.</p><p>Repairs handled and the house needs a clean next? See the Omaha house cleaning cost guide: <a href=\"/blog/how-much-does-house-cleaning-cost-in-omaha/\">How much does house cleaning cost in Omaha?</a></p>{{img:2}}'
