      tldr: 'In Denver, a handyman runs $50 to $90 an hour on HomeBlue, with 4 hours at $190 to $350 and 8 hours at $370 to $700, plus materials. Thumbtack puts Denver at $40 to more than $100 an hour, with minimums usually $60 to $100. Two metro operators post flat rates inside those ranges — $80 an hour and $75 an hour. One small job alone almost always pays a minimum. A list bundled into one visit is where your money stretches.',
      faq: [
        { q: 'How much does a handyman cost in Denver?', a: '$50 to $90 an hour on HomeBlue’s Denver guide, and $40 to more than $100 an hour on Thumbtack’s Denver page. Four hours runs $190 to $350 on HomeBlue. Two Denver metro operators post $75 and $80 an hour flat.' },
        { q: 'How much is a half day or a full day in Denver?', a: 'HomeBlue puts 8 hours at $370 to $700. EvenQuote puts a Denver half day at $300 to $490 and a full day at $560 to $920, with the higher figures tied to local companies and franchises rather than independents.' },
        { q: 'Why did my small job cost more than the hourly rate?', a: 'Minimums. Thumbtack puts typical minimums at $60 to $100 in Denver, and EvenQuote puts most minimums at $75 to $200, sometimes plus a $30 to $80 trip charge. A 20-minute fix booked alone still pays for the drive and the first hour.' },
        { q: 'Can a handyman do plumbing or electrical work in Denver?', a: 'Small swaps like a faucet or a light fixture are priced as flat handyman jobs on Thumbtack’s Denver page — $125 and $75. Bigger trade work is different: Colorado requires licensing for electrical, plumbing, and HVAC work, so ask who is licensed for the job you are booking.' },
        { q: 'How do I find a handyman near me in Denver?', a: 'Search Van Squads by ZIP. All 7 listed handyman businesses cover Aurora (80013), in the Denver metro.' }
      ],
      body: '{{img:0}}<h3>What Denver homeowners actually pay</h3><p>Prices from Denver pages, plus one national row labeled national:</p><table class="price-table"><thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Typical price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Handyman, per hour, Denver (HomeBlue)</td><td>$50–$90</td></tr><tr><td>Handyman, per hour, Denver (Thumbtack)</td><td>$40 to $100+</td></tr><tr><td>Handyman, per hour, Denver metro operator (Denver Handy Co.)</td><td>$80 flat</td></tr><tr><td>Handyman, per hour, Denver metro operator serving Aurora (Handy Andy)</td><td>$75 ($65 if you mention the website)</td></tr><tr><td>Independent handyman, per hour, Denver (EvenQuote)</td><td>$60–$88</td></tr><tr><td>Local company, per hour, Denver (EvenQuote)</td><td>$88–$120</td></tr><tr><td>4 hours of work, Denver (HomeBlue)</td><td>$190–$350</td></tr><tr><td>Half day, Denver (EvenQuote)</td><td>$300–$490</td></tr><tr><td>8 hours of work, Denver (HomeBlue)</td><td>$370–$700</td></tr><tr><td>Full day, Denver (EvenQuote)</td><td>$560–$920</td></tr><tr><td>Faucet replacement, flat rate (Thumbtack, Denver page)</td><td>$125</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling fan or light fixture, flat rate (Thumbtack, Denver page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Toilet replacement, flat rate (Thumbtack, Denver page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Attic ladder replacement, flat rate (Thumbtack, Denver page)</td><td>$150</td></tr><tr><td>TV mounting (HomeGuide via EvenQuote, national)</td><td>$100–$400</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is where people overpay. A $75 toilet swap sounds cheap until you book it alone and the $60 to $100 minimum is the real price — plus a trip charge if that shop adds one. EvenQuote puts most minimums at $75 to $200 nationally, with a $30 to $80 trip charge on top at some shops. Put the toilet, the faucet, and the patch on one list and the minimum stops eating the visit.</p>{{img:1}}<h3>What changes the price in Denver?</h3><p><strong>The type of shop sets the hourly band.</strong> EvenQuote splits Denver cleanly: an independent runs $60 to $88 an hour, a local company $88 to $120, and a franchise $100 to $155. HomeBlue’s $50 to $90 range covers the independent end of that same market. You’re not always buying better work at the top band. Sometimes you’re buying a call center and a warranty desk.</p><p><strong>Front Range weather writes a lot of the list.</strong> One metro operator with 15+ years here puts it this way: Front Range winters, hail seasons, and bone-dry summers are what a Colorado house has to hold together against. In Aurora (80013), that shows up as cracked caulk, loose fence pickets, and doors that fit in May and stick by January. All one-hour jobs — if they’re on the same list.</p><p><strong>Materials sit outside the hourly rate.</strong> HomeBlue’s Denver table is labor only. Both metro operators above bill materials at cost, with no markup and no travel fee in Aurora. Ask before anyone starts: what’s the hourly rate, and how are materials billed?</p><p><strong>Licensed work and permits push a job out of handyman pricing.</strong> HomeBlue notes your price rises if the task needs a trade license, special equipment, or a permit. That changes who can legally do the work, and what the visit costs.</p><h3>What can a handyman legally do in Denver?</h3><p>Colorado doesn’t require a state handyman license for general repair and maintenance work. Denver Handy Co. spells out the other half: licensing is required for electrical, plumbing, and HVAC work, and for projects that need permits.</p><p>HomeBlue gives two permit examples worth knowing: an exterior door replacement sometimes needs a permit in Denver, and decks of a certain height and size can need one too. A faucet swap, a patch, a curtain rod — that’s handyman work. New wiring or a moved drain isn’t, no matter how small it looks on your list.</p><h3>How to pay less and still get it done right</h3><ul class="check-list"><li><strong>Hand over the whole list at once.</strong> Handy Andy says most customers stack several small jobs into one visit, and with hourly billing that’s the cheapest way to clear a list. His typical times help you build one — a leaky faucet is 1 to 2 hours, flat-pack assembly 1 to 3, a patch with paint touch-up 1 to 4.</li><li><strong>Use the minimum, don’t fight it.</strong> If the visit minimum is one hour, have a second job ready. A $125 faucet plus a second small fix inside the same hour costs the same as the faucet alone.</li><li><strong>Send photos before the visit.</strong> Denver Handy Co. says estimates scoped from a description and photos usually land within 10 to 15% of the actual time. A photo beats a surprise after the wall is open.</li><li><strong>Ask about travel before you book.</strong> Two metro operators charge no travel fee in Aurora. If a quote from farther out adds one, that’s a real price difference.</li></ul><h3>A Denver handyman business listed on Van Squads</h3><p><strong>Grandma’s Handyman Service</strong> is listed on Van Squads, offering handyman work at your location in Aurora (80013). See its listing: <a href="/business/grandmas-handyman-service">Grandma’s Handyman Service on Van Squads</a>.</p><p>Van Squads lists 7 handyman businesses in the Denver metro, all covering Aurora (80013): <a href="/#/browse?zip=80013">search handyman near 80013</a>.</p><p>Want the whole list on one page? <a href="/handyman/denver/">Handyman in Denver</a> has every company Van Squads lists there, with phone numbers and service areas.</p><p>Home needs cleaning too? See the Denver house cleaning cost guide: <a href=\"/blog/how-much-does-house-cleaning-cost-in-denver/\">How much does house cleaning cost in Denver?</a></p><p>Comparing metros? See the Denver pressure washing cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-pressure-washing-cost-in-denver/">How much does pressure washing cost in Denver?</a> and the Denver mobile car detailing cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-mobile-car-detailing-cost-in-denver/">How much does mobile car detailing cost in Denver?</a></p>{{img:2}}'
