      body: '{{img:0}}<h3>What Tulsa homeowners actually pay</h3><p>Prices from Tulsa pages and operators, plus one national row labeled national:</p><table class="price-table"><thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Typical price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>House wash, 1,500–2,000 sq ft house, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$400–$600 (avg ~$500)</td></tr><tr><td>House wash, 2,000–3,000 sq ft house, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$500–$1,000</td></tr><tr><td>House wash, 3,000–4,000 sq ft house, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$700–$1,300</td></tr><tr><td>House wash, 4,000–5,000 sq ft house, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$900–$1,600</td></tr><tr><td>Driveway, 40 x 20 ft, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$150–$260 (avg ~$200)</td></tr><tr><td>Driveway, two-car, Tulsa operator (C&S Home Services)</td><td>$200 avg</td></tr><tr><td>Driveway, three-car, Tulsa operator (C&S Home Services)</td><td>$250 avg</td></tr><tr><td>Driveway, 60 x 20 ft, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$220–$380</td></tr><tr><td>Deck or patio, 200 sq ft, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$60–$100 (avg ~$80)</td></tr><tr><td>Deck or patio, 600 sq ft, Tulsa (HomeBlue)</td><td>$190–$300</td></tr><tr><td>Standard project, Tulsa row (HouseProjectCalc)</td><td>$175–$525</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure washing, per sq ft of property size, Tulsa (Thumbtack)</td><td>$0.10–$0.17</td></tr><tr><td>Tulsa pressure washing / driveway, Tulsa County page (Aptera)</td><td>$436 / $405</td></tr><tr><td>House / siding wash (HomeGuide, national)</td><td>$170–$360</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is where people overpay. HomeBlue and a Tulsa operator agree a normal driveway sits near $200. Aptera\'s Tulsa County page puts a Tulsa driveway at $405 — double that, for the same city. The work didn\'t change. The scope or the measuring stick did. Before you pay the higher number, ask what it covers: driveway only, or driveway, walk, and curb in one job? And whether a square-foot price measures the surface washed or your whole property. Thumbtack\'s Tulsa figure uses the second one.</p>{{img:1}}<h3>What changes the price in Tulsa?</h3><p><strong>Red clay doesn\'t rinse off like loose dirt.</strong> A Bixby operator who washes across the Tulsa metro puts it plainly: red clay bonds to porous concrete through its iron oxide pigment instead of sitting on top. A heavy stain often needs a pre-treatment soak or a second targeted pass. That\'s paid time on a quote, and why a clay-stained drive in Midtown can price above a plain $200 wash.</p><p><strong>Rain and muggy summers feed the green film.</strong> Tulsa\'s wettest month is May, at 5.0 inches of rain on average, and July is muggy most of the time on WeatherSpark\'s averages. A Tulsa handyman company\'s page says washing removes mold, mildew, and algae, but in humid conditions it returns over time. Shaded north walls in Maple Ridge or Brookside grow it first.</p><p><strong>Wind puts dust back on the house.</strong> Squeegee Squad\'s Tulsa guide notes Oklahoma ranks among the windiest states, and strong winds carry dust, pollen, and road grime across the city. On concrete, that dust mixes with clay splash at the driveway edge.</p><p><strong>Size and stories set the base price.</strong> HomeBlue climbs by house size, up to $900 to $1,600 at 4,000 to 5,000 sq ft. Two or more stories, tight access, and limited water access push it up. Heated water — power washing, strictly speaking — adds 10 to 15% over HomeBlue\'s cold-water prices. It earns it on oil and grease, not on clay dust.</p><h3>Why older Tulsa homes get a soft wash, not a blast</h3><p>The method matters, and Tulsa has a lot of older housing. A Tulsa cost page using Census-derived figures notes 53% of owner-occupied homes here were built from 1950 to 1979 — the brick and older siding in Cherry Street, Kendall Whittier, and Florence Park. The Bixby operator\'s rule fits that stock: concrete gets 2,500 to 3,500 PSI, while siding, roofing, and painted surfaces get a soft wash. If a quote doesn\'t name the method, ask.</p><h3>How to pay less and still get it clean</h3><ul class="check-list"><li><strong>Bundle surfaces in one visit.</strong> Driveway, front walk, patio — one stop. A 200 sq ft deck or patio runs $60 to $100 as its own job on HomeBlue; as an add-on it should cost less.</li><li><strong>Send photos of the worst spots.</strong> The red clay tint at the driveway edge, the green north wall, the oil spot by the garage. The Tulsa operator above prices driveways from a texted photo.</li><li><strong>Ask what the square-foot price measures.</strong> Surface washed, or your whole property? The two numbers aren\'t close.</li><li><strong>Wash after the spring rains, not before them.</strong> May is Tulsa\'s wettest month. A wash right after that stretch clears pollen and storm film in one go.</li></ul><h3>A Tulsa pressure washing business listed on Van Squads</h3><p><strong>H2O Tulsa</strong> is listed on Van Squads, offering pressure washing at your location in Tulsa (74105). See its listing: <a href="/business/h2o-tulsa">H2O Tulsa on Van Squads</a>.</p><p>Van Squads lists 6 pressure washing businesses in the Tulsa metro, all covering Tulsa ZIP 74105: <a href="/#/browse?zip=74105">search pressure washing near 74105</a>.</p><p>Want the whole list on one page? <a href="/pressure-washing/tulsa/">Pressure washing in Tulsa</a> has every company Van Squads lists there, with phone numbers and service areas.</p><p>House due for a clean too? See the Tulsa house cleaning cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-house-cleaning-cost-in-tulsa/">How much does house cleaning cost in Tulsa?</a></p><p>Repairs on the list too? See the Tulsa handyman cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-handyman-cost-in-tulsa/">How much does a handyman cost in Tulsa?</a></p><p>Comparing metros? See the Salt Lake City pressure washing cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-pressure-washing-cost-in-salt-lake-city/">How much does pressure washing cost in Salt Lake City?</a></p>{{img:2}}'
