      body: '{{img:0}}<h3>What Tulsa dog owners actually pay</h3><p>Prices from Tulsa and Broken Arrow pages, plus a national figure labeled national:</p><table class="price-table"><thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Typical price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mobile full service, average-size dog (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>About $75</td></tr><tr><td>Haircut, mobile operator serving Greater Tulsa (Furst Class Canine)</td><td>$80–$150</td></tr><tr><td>Bath, mobile operator serving Greater Tulsa (Furst Class Canine)</td><td>$65–$150</td></tr><tr><td>Full groom, medium dog, starting range (Broken Arrow guide, groomers within 25 miles)</td><td>$70–$84</td></tr><tr><td>Full service, small dog, salon starting price (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$40</td></tr><tr><td>Full service, large dog, salon starting price (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Nail trim (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$10</td></tr><tr><td>Nail trim + buffing (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$15</td></tr><tr><td>Teeth brushing (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$10</td></tr><tr><td>Ear cleaning (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$10</td></tr><tr><td>Flea & tick treatment (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$15</td></tr><tr><td>Face, feet and fanny trim (HomeGuide, Tulsa page)</td><td>$15</td></tr><tr><td>Pet groomer, individual, average (Care.com, Tulsa)</td><td>$12.90/hr</td></tr><tr><td>Dog grooming, national average range (Thumbtack, national)</td><td>$95–$160</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is where people overpay. A $75 mobile visit and a $150 mobile visit can both be honest quotes in Tulsa. The first is an average-size dog with a coat in good shape. The second is a big dog, a long coat, or matting — Furst Class Canine says severe matting and special handling add fees on top of its ranges. Ask what counts as an add-on before you compare totals.</p>{{img:1}}<h3>What changes the price in Tulsa?</h3><p><strong>Size first, coat second.</strong> HomeGuide\'s Tulsa page starts a salon full service at $40 for a small dog and $75 for a large one, and the mobile operator serving Greater Tulsa spreads its baths across $65–$150 by weight and service. A 12-pound dog is a short visit. A 90-pound double coat isn\'t.</p><p><strong>Matting and coat condition.</strong> A dog on a regular schedule grooms faster than a dog who hasn\'t seen a brush in months. That time is the price, whether it\'s written as a flat range or a base price plus a matting fee.</p><p><strong>Flea and tick season runs long here.</strong> Nutri-Green\'s Tulsa guide puts flea and tick season in Tulsa from April through October, peaking in late spring and summer once temperatures stay above 70°F. HomeGuide\'s Tulsa page prices a flea & tick treatment at $15 as an add-on. A dog who spends spring in a Broken Arrow backyard near a fence line or under a deck — the shaded spots Nutri-Green names — needs that add-on priced in, not sprung on you at the van.</p><p><strong>One dog at a time costs more than a salon line.</strong> A salon runs several dogs through a day, hours apart. A van takes your dog start to finish, at your curb. You pay for the drive, the van, and the solo time.</p><h3>Mobile or salon — what does the extra money buy?</h3><p>A salon full service in Tulsa starts at $40 for a small dog and $75 for a large dog on HomeGuide\'s Tulsa page. The same page puts a mobile full service at about $75 for an average-size dog, and Thumbtack\'s national guide says mobile prices run higher because the van does the full job at your location.</p><p>What you get for it: no car ride with a nervous dog, no cage wait, and a dog who walks back into your own house when the cut is done. For an older dog, a dog who hates the car, or a house in Broken Arrow (74012) where the salon run eats half a morning, that difference is the point. For a calm dog and a tight budget, the salon still wins on price.</p><h3>How to pay less and still get a good groom</h3><ul class="check-list"><li><strong>Stay on a regular schedule.</strong> Care.com\'s Tulsa page notes that regular grooming keeps a coat in shape and cuts shedding and matting. A dog seen every few weeks is a shorter visit to quote.</li><li><strong>Brush at home between visits.</strong> Loose undercoat and small tangles are free to fix on your porch. Matting at the van isn\'t.</li><li><strong>Price the add-ons up front.</strong> On HomeGuide\'s Tulsa list the small pieces are cheap alone — nails $10, teeth $10, ears $10 — but they add up if each one is a surprise.</li><li><strong>Tip on the work.</strong> HomeGuide\'s Tulsa page puts a normal tip at 15 to 20 percent of the total, with more for a dog who was extremely dirty or heavily matted.</li></ul><h3>A Tulsa mobile pet grooming business listed on Van Squads</h3><p><strong>Bathewell Mobile Pet Spa</strong> is listed on Van Squads, offering mobile pet grooming at your location in Broken Arrow ZIP 74012, in the Tulsa metro. See its listing: <a href="/business/bathewell-mobile-pet-spa">Bathewell Mobile Pet Spa on Van Squads</a>.</p><p>Van Squads lists 3 mobile pet grooming businesses in the Tulsa metro, all covering ZIP 74012: <a href="/#/browse?zip=74012">search mobile pet grooming near 74012</a>.</p><p>Want the whole list on one page? <a href="/mobile-pet-grooming/tulsa/">Mobile pet grooming in Tulsa</a> has every company Van Squads lists there, with phone numbers and service areas.</p><p>Got a house to go with the dog? See the Tulsa house cleaning cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-house-cleaning-cost-in-tulsa/">How much does house cleaning cost in Tulsa?</a> and the Tulsa deep cleaning cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-deep-cleaning-cost-in-tulsa/">How much does deep cleaning cost in Tulsa?</a></p><p>Comparing metros? See the Salt Lake City mobile pet grooming cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-mobile-pet-grooming-cost-in-salt-lake-city/">How much does mobile pet grooming cost in Salt Lake City?</a></p>{{img:2}}'
