      body: '{{img:0}}<h3>What Fargo homeowners actually pay</h3><p>Prices from Fargo and Fargo-metro pages, plus national figures labeled national:</p><table class="price-table"><thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Typical price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hourly rate (HomeBlue, Fargo)</td><td>$40–$80</td></tr><tr><td>1-hour task (HomeBlue, Fargo)</td><td>$40–$80</td></tr><tr><td>2-hour task (HomeBlue, Fargo)</td><td>$90–$170</td></tr><tr><td>4-hour task, half day (HomeBlue, Fargo)</td><td>$180–$330</td></tr><tr><td>8-hour task, full day (HomeBlue, Fargo)</td><td>$350–$670</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum charge (Thumbtack, West Fargo page)</td><td>$60–$100</td></tr><tr><td>Toilet replacement, flat (Thumbtack, West Fargo page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling fan or light fixture, flat (Thumbtack, West Fargo page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Garbage disposal, flat (Thumbtack, West Fargo page)</td><td>$75</td></tr><tr><td>Faucet replacement, flat (Thumbtack, West Fargo page)</td><td>$125</td></tr><tr><td>Attic ladder replacement, flat (Thumbtack, West Fargo page)</td><td>$150</td></tr><tr><td>Gutter cleaning, typical 1–2 story home (HomeBlue, Fargo)</td><td>$70–$180</td></tr><tr><td>North Dakota hourly, state estimate (FastEstimateMaker)</td><td>$55–$78</td></tr><tr><td>Typical handyman project, national (Angi, national)</td><td>$405 avg ($164–$647)</td></tr><tr><td>Hourly, self-employed / company, national (HomeGuide via Housecall Pro, national)</td><td>$50–$80 / $75–$125</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is where people overpay. One small job on its own pays the minimum and the trip. The same job on a half-day list just takes its share of the $180 to $330. A faucet at $125 flat, a toilet at $75 flat — those are fine prices for a single visit, but a list of four small jobs priced one by one can cost more than the half day that covers all four.</p>{{img:1}}<h3>What changes the price in Fargo?</h3><p><strong>How the visit is priced matters more than the job itself.</strong> HomeBlue’s Fargo page prices by the hour, from $40–$80 for one hour up to $350–$670 for eight, and the per-hour cost drops as the block gets longer — four hours at $180–$330 isn’t four times the one-hour price. Thumbtack’s West Fargo page makes the same point from the other side: bundle your tasks into one visit and you make the most of the minimum fee.</p><p><strong>November is list season in Fargo.</strong> The work that shows up before the cold sets in is predictable: a storm door that won’t latch, drafts around windows and doors, gutters that still need cleaning before anything freezes in them. Gutter cleaning in Fargo runs $70 to $180 for a typical one- or two-story home on HomeBlue’s Fargo guide. None of that’s big work. All of it costs more as an emergency call in January than as a line on a November list.</p><p><strong>South Fargo is where the directory coverage sits.</strong> Every Fargo handyman listing on Van Squads covers ZIP 58104 — the south side of the city, Rose Creek and Osgood included. Punch lists in those neighborhoods tend to be the same mix: a sticking door, a patch, a fixture swap, trim that needs attention. If you’re in 58104, you have 8 businesses to compare. Get two quotes on the same written list, not on two different versions of it.</p><p><strong>Big jobs cross a state line — a dollar one.</strong> North Dakota has no handyman license as such, but the Secretary of State is clear: when the cost, value, or price of a job goes over $4,000, the person doing the work must hold a state contractor license. Plumbing and electrical work sit on their own license tracks at any price. A $300 list is handyman work. A $5,000 project is a contractor conversation, and the license is the first thing to ask about.</p><h3>How to pay less and still get the work done</h3><ul class="check-list"><li><strong>Write the whole list before you call.</strong> Doors, patches, fixtures, the lot. One visit, one minimum, one trip across town instead of three.</li><li><strong>Ask which jobs price flat.</strong> A toilet at $75 flat and a faucet at $125 flat can beat an open hourly clock. Ask before the work starts, not after.</li><li><strong>Supply the parts when you can.</strong> HomeBlue’s Fargo prices are labor — materials sit on top. A faucet or fixture already in the house saves a shopping trip billed as time.</li><li><strong>Stay flexible on timing.</strong> HomeBlue’s Fargo page lists flexible scheduling and one-day jobs among the things that pull a price down, and emergency calls and permit work among the things that push it up.</li></ul><h3>A Fargo handyman business listed on Van Squads</h3><p><strong>Fargo Handyman LLC</strong> is listed on Van Squads, offering handyman work at your location in Fargo ZIP 58104. See its listing: <a href="/business/fargo-handyman-llc">Fargo Handyman LLC on Van Squads</a>.</p><p>Van Squads lists 8 handyman businesses in the Fargo metro, covering ZIP 58104: <a href="/#/browse?zip=58104">search handyman near 58104</a>.</p><p>Want the whole list on one page? <a href="/handyman/fargo/">Handyman in Fargo</a> has every company Van Squads lists there, with phone numbers and service areas.</p><p>House due for a clean too? See the Fargo house cleaning cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-house-cleaning-cost-in-fargo/">How much does house cleaning cost in Fargo?</a></p><p>Comparing metros? See the Tulsa handyman cost guide: <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-handyman-cost-in-tulsa/">How much does a handyman cost in Tulsa?</a></p>{{img:2}}'
